    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and all 4 tests pass (the 2 existing ones plus 2 new ones).\n\n**How it works:** it connects to the server with the mcp SDK's own `mcp.Client(server, mode=\"legacy\", cache=None)`, which runs the server in memory with no subprocess or sockets. Then it follows `check_command` step for step: `ClientSession` \u2192 `initialize()` \u2192 `session.call_tool(case.tool, case.arguments)` for each case in order \u2192 the same `compare()`. So mismatches come back the same way and in the same order as over stdio. No dependencies were added.\n\n- **`mode=\"legacy\"`:** without it, mcp 2.2.0 uses a newer direct connection for in-process servers that skips the initialize handshake. Legacy mode keeps the same handshake `check_command` uses.\n- **`cache=None`:** this turns off the client's response caching, so every call goes to the server as it would over stdio.\n\n`check_server` accepts servers built with `MCPServer`, the SDK's decorator-based server class, and also the lower-level `Server`.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_inproc.py`, which builds a two-tool `MCPServer` and checks two things: a set of cases that all pass, and that mismatches are reported in order with the right output. The tests call `anyio.run` directly, so they don't need an async pytest plugin.\n\n**API lookup:** CLAUDE.md asks for a Context7 lookup before using a library's API, but Context7 returned \"Monthly quota exceeded\". Instead, I read the installed mcp 2.2.0 source (`mcp/client/client.py`, `mcp/client/_memory.py`) to confirm the calls I used."
  },
